1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 There was this and this happens to all of us. No blame there. But it's not great if, by default, a server spins up. CORS headers are set in such a way that any website you open in your browser can now access your open code server.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 Entirely unrelated to all of this, I started looking into benchmarks for coding agent harnesses and found Terminal-Bench, which is a pretty good benchmark, all things considered.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 And the funny part about it is that it's the most minimal kind of thing you can think of. All it gives the model is a tool to send keystrokes to a tmux session and read the output of that tmux session.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 There's no file tools, no sub-agents, none of that stuff. And it's one of the best performing harnesses in the leaderboard.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 Here's the leaderboard from December 2025. Irrespective of model family, Terminal scores higher. Mostly even higher than the native harness of that model.

6
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 So what does that tell us? A form two thesis is: We are in the "fuck around and find out" phase of coding agents, and their current form is not their final form, right?

7
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 So, second thesis is: We need better ways to fuck around. And for me that means self-modifying malleable agents.

8
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Things that the agent itself can modify, and I can modify, depending on my workflow.

9
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 So I stripped away all the things, built a minimal core, but made it super extensible, and made it so that the agent can modify itself.

10
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 With some creature comforts. It's not entirely barebones.

11
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 So that's Pi. It's an agent that adapts to your workflow instead of the other way around.

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 It comes with four packages. An AI package, which is basically just an abstraction across providers, and context handoff between providers.

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 An agent core, which is just a while loop and a tool calling.

14
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
 A bespoke 2E framework. I come out of game development, so I built a thing that actually doesn't flicker too much.

15
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 And the coding agent itself.

16
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 Here's Pi's system prompt.

17
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 And that's it.

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,020
 Eventually the industry created a new standard called skills, which is basically just markdown files.

